      mobile:pt-60 mobile:flex mobile:flex-col mobile:items-center"
    >
      <!-- Header -->
      <div class="flex gap-4 flex-col blog-container">
        <h2
          class="text-dark desktop:text-left smalldesktop:text-left mobile:text-left tablet:text-left font-bold"
        >
          Astro + Vue component
        </h2>
        <h4 class="font-bold italic">
          <span
            class="humana-gradient text-dark/0 bg-clip-text desktop:text-left smalldesktop:text-left tablet:text-left mobile:text-left"
            >Astro <br
              class="desktop:hidden tablet:hidden smalldesktop:hidden mobile:block"
            />Vue</span
          >
        </h4>
        <p
          class="opacity-80 text-dark desktop:text-left mobile:text-left smalldesktop:text-left tablet:text-left"
        >
          Couple of weeks ago, I jumped into building my portfolio website using
          astro. I wrestled with the question of which frameworks to use, as I
          have previously used Vue & Angular for some of the projects. Doom
          scrolling through X on a lazy afternoon, I was persuaded by the hype
          generated by devs like primegen (on youtube) and Fred Schkott (CEO of
          HTML). The claim of picking the choice of your weapon for the battle
          seemed almost outworldly for someone like me who is into their third
          year of programming career. Hence this article is an effort to try out
          the ultimate promise and end the ancient discussion once and for all.
          Lets see how it goes!
        </p>
      </div>

