        smalldesktop:sticky smalldesktop:top-20 smalldesktop:-inset-20 smalldesktop:z-10 smalldesktop:flex
        tablet:hidden
        mobile:hidden"
        >
      <ul
        class="desktop:flex desktop:absolute desktop:-left-52 desktop:text-sm desktop:flex-col desktop:gap-2 desktop:text-dark
      smalldesktop:flex smalldesktop:absolute smalldesktop:-left-32 smalldesktop:text-sm smalldesktop:flex-col smalldesktop:gap-2 smalldesktop:text-dark"
      >
        <li class="font-bold leading-10 tracking-widest">TABLE OF CONTENTS</li>
        <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
        <li><a href="#drop-down">Drop-down component</a></li>
        <li style="margin-left:10px">
          <a href="#breakdown">Technical breakdown</a>
        </li>
        <li style="margin-left:10px"><a href="#snippets">Code snippets</a></li>
        <li><a href="#choice">Challenges</a></li>
        <li><a href="#needs">Missing features</a></li>
        <li><a href="#future-works">Future works</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Overview section -->
    <section
      id="overview"
      class="flex flex-col desktop:my-2 smalldesktop:my-2 tablet:my-5 mobile:my-8"
    >
      <BlogSection
        title="Overview"
        description=`A couple of weeks ago, I jumped into building my portfolio website using Astro. I wrestled with the question of which 
        frameworks to use, as I have previously used Vue & Angular for some of the projects. Doom scrolling through X on a lazy afternoon, 
        I was persuaded by the hype generated by devs like Primegen (on YouTube) and Fred Schkott (CEO of HTML). The claim of picking the 
        choice of your weapon for the battle seemed almost outworldly for someone like me who is into their third year of programming career. 
        Hence this article is an effort to try out the ultimate promise and end the ancient discussion once and for all. Let us see how it goes!`
      />
      <BlogSection
        description=`In this article, I document my experience of building a custom 
        drop-down menu shown in the video using Vue and Astro, with some code snippets to steal.`
      />
    </section>

    <!-- Drop Down section -->
    <section
      id="drop-down"
      class="flex flex-col desktop:my-2 smalldesktop:my-2 tablet:my-5 mobile:my-8"
    >
      <BlogSection
        title="The component"
        description=`The purpose of the drop-down component is for the user to pick the person to who they intend to wire the money. 
        The options are presented to the user in a chip-like format, where the chip anatomy of the chip is essentially the user’s picture 
        and their name wrapped in a thinly bordered container. `
      />

      <BlogSection
        description=`As the user clicks on the select field, a drop-down menu opens and the user is presented with all the possible options at
        their disposal by the system. Finally, the user is prompted to select one of the options and move on to the rest of the input fields.`
      />
    </section>

    <!-- Technical breakdown -->
    <section
      id="breakdown"
      class="flex flex-col desktop:my-5 smalldesktop:my-5 tablet:my-5 mobile:my-8"
    >
      <BlogSection
        subtitle="Technical breakdown"
        description=` The component follows a simple notion of feeding a dataset to the component, 
        processing the data through the component logic with the designed interaction, and eventually 
        returning the result after the interaction.`
      />
    </section>
    <!-- On a code level -->
    <!-- <section
      id="code"
      class="flex flex-col desktop:my-5 smalldesktop:my-5 tablet:my-5 mobile:my-8"
    >
      <BlogSection subtitle="On code level" />
    </section> -->
